::ENG3_U04_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An article explains that school murals preserve local history through interviews, neighborhood scenes, and captions. Which subject statement is most accurate? {
~The article is about students painting walls.#This is too narrow.
=The article is about school murals as a way to preserve local history.#Correct. This accurately names the subject.
~The article is about captions only.#This focuses on one detail.
~The article is about why all schools need murals.#This overstates the summary.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A mural shows a closed bakery, a streetcar line, and a family porch. Which claim best analyzes the mural's literary or symbolic role in the article summary? {
~The mural has three pictures.#This is summary.
~The mural is painted on a wall.#This is a literal detail.
=The mural symbolizes community memory by preserving places connected to everyday life.#Correct. This makes an analytical claim.
~The mural should include brighter colors.#This is an unsupported suggestion.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Draft: The mural keeps old stuff. Which revision uses precise academic vocabulary? {
~The mural has a lot of old things.#This is vague.
~The mural is about stuff from before.#This is informal.
~The mural is nice because it remembers things.#This is vague.
=The mural preserves historical memory through public images of neighborhood life.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The article is about gardens and culture and food and it is important. Which revision improves clarity? {
=The article presents community gardens as spaces that meet practical food needs while preserving cultural memory.#Correct. This is clear and precise.
~The article is about food and culture and gardens being important.#This remains list-like.
~Gardens are important because food and culture.#This is not a complete academic sentence.
~The article has gardens, culture, and food stuff.#This is informal.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report uses headings for books, internet access, job help, and meeting rooms before arguing that libraries are civic infrastructure. Which analysis best connects subject and organization? {
~The report has headings.#This is summary only.
~Libraries have many services.#This names a topic but not organization.
=The headings organize services separately so readers can understand how libraries function as civic infrastructure.#Correct. This connects structure and subject.
~The meeting rooms are probably the most important service.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An essay begins with an empty bus stop, then gives examples of riders who depend on public transit, and ends by arguing that transportation shapes opportunity. Which claim best shows subject understanding? {
~The essay is about buses.#This is too narrow.
~The essay is about people waiting.#This misses the broader subject.
~The essay has an empty bus stop at the beginning.#This is summary.
=The essay presents public transportation as a system that affects access to opportunity.#Correct. This accurately captures the subject.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The bus article is cool because it shows people need rides. Which revision uses appropriate academic voice? {
~The bus article is pretty serious because rides matter.#This remains informal.
=The article emphasizes that public transportation affects daily access to work, school, and community resources.#Correct. This is formal and precise.
~The article is cool and important to people.#This is vague.
~People need rides, and the article gets that.#This is informal.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Draft: The essay blank the subject of public transportation by showing how riders depend on it for daily opportunity. Which word best completes the sentence? {
~mentions#This is too weak for analysis.
~likes#This is not academic.
=develops#Correct. Develops precisely explains how the essay builds the subject.
~guesses#This does not fit the context.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report first defines food insecurity, then gives local data, then describes school pantry programs. Which organization analysis is strongest? {
=The report moves from definition to data to response, helping readers understand the problem before seeing a possible solution.#Correct. This explains logical organization.
~The report is about food.#This is too broad.
~The report has three parts.#This is true but not analytical.
~The pantry programs should come first in every report.#This is an unsupported rule.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: Students record family recipes beside mural images of neighborhood stores. Which literary analysis claim is strongest? {
~Students write recipes.#This is summary.
~Stores sell food.#This is general information.
~The mural has images and words.#This describes features.
=The recipes and store images work together to show cultural memory as both personal and public.#Correct. This analyzes meaning.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Scenario: A paragraph has accurate subject understanding but repeats the same idea in three sentences. What revision is most needed? {
~Add unrelated examples.#This would weaken focus.
~Remove the subject entirely.#The subject understanding is a strength.
=Combine repeated ideas and add clearer elaboration.#Correct. Revision should improve clarity and structure.
~Use more informal language.#This would weaken academic voice.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An article about public art begins with a student interview, then explains mural planning, then ends with community response. Which claim best analyzes structure? {
=The article moves from personal voice to process to community impact, showing how individual participation becomes public memory.#Correct. This analyzes organization and subject.
~The article has a student interview.#This is summary only.
~The mural planning comes after the interview.#This describes order without explaining effect.
~The community response should be removed.#This is an unsupported preference.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Scenario: A student is revising a complex text analysis paragraph. Which phrase best fits academic analysis? {
~a bunch of stuff#This is informal and vague.
~really gets the point across#This is informal.
=clarifies the subject#Correct. This phrase is precise and academic.
~kind of shows things#This is vague.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report explains library services with headings and ends by calling libraries civic infrastructure. Which detail best shows subject understanding? {
~The report has headings.#This is structural but does not fully show subject understanding.
=The report connects library services to community needs.#Correct. This shows understanding of the subject.
~The report is probably long.#This is unsupported.
~The report has words about libraries.#This is vague.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: Public art preserves history because the article says murals and interviews and places. Which revision is clearest? {
~Public art preserves history because murals and interviews and places are there.#This is awkward and vague.
~The article has murals, interviews, and places, which are history.#This is unclear.
=The article shows that public art preserves history by combining mural images, interviews, and named neighborhood places.#Correct. This is clear and specific.
~Public art does history because of the stuff in the article.#This is informal.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Scenario: A student writes, The text is about public art. It is good. What is missing? {
~A casual tone#Academic analysis does not need casual tone.
~A longer title#The issue is not title length.
~A personal opinion#The sentence already relies too much on opinion.
=Specific subject understanding and analysis of how the text develops the subject#Correct. The statement is too vague.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A mural includes a faded train station beside new apartment buildings. Which claim best analyzes contrast? {
~The mural has old and new buildings.#This is summary only.
~The apartment buildings are newer than the station.#This is literal.
=The contrast between the station and apartments suggests a neighborhood shaped by memory and change.#Correct. This analyzes contrast.
~The mural should focus only on the station.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Draft: The article talks about how the garden helps people remember culture. Which revision uses stronger academic vocabulary? {
=The article emphasizes the garden's role in preserving cultural memory.#Correct. This is concise and academic.
~The article says the garden remembers culture stuff.#This is informal.
~The garden is about remembering things in culture.#This is vague.
~The article has culture and memory.#This is not a full analysis.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report defines civic infrastructure, lists library services, and concludes that libraries are civic infrastructure. Which organization claim is strongest? {
~The report repeats the word library.#This is not meaningful structure analysis.
~The report has a conclusion.#This is too general.
~The services are listed in the middle.#This is descriptive only.
=The report defines the concept before showing how library services fit that concept.#Correct. This explains organization and subject development.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Scenario: A student's analysis has a strong claim but uses vague words like things, stuff, and good. What revision should the student make? {
~Add more vague words for consistency.#This would not improve clarity.
~Remove the claim.#The claim is strong.
=Replace vague words with precise academic vocabulary.#Correct. This improves style and clarity.
~Use only personal opinion.#Academic analysis requires evidence and precision.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An essay about community gardens begins with food access, then cultural memory, then a final claim about public spaces. Which analysis best shows subject understanding? {
~The essay is about plants.#This is too narrow.
=The essay explains community gardens as public spaces that serve both material and cultural needs.#Correct. This captures the subject accurately.
~The essay has three parts.#This is too general.
~The essay says food is better than memory.#This is unsupported.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A public mural shows hands passing seeds from one generation to another. Which claim is strongest? {
~The mural has hands and seeds.#This is summary.
~Seeds can grow into plants.#This is literal.
~The hands are probably painted large.#This is unsupported.
=The passing seeds symbolize cultural inheritance across generations.#Correct. This interprets the image.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Scenario: A student analyzes a complex article for classmates and a teacher. Which tone is appropriate? {
~Sarcastic and casual#This does not fit academic analysis.
=Formal, focused, and explanatory#Correct. This fits the task and audience.
~Slang-heavy and humorous#This is too informal.
~Angry and exaggerated#This is not appropriate.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The headings show stuff about library services. Which revision is best? {
~The headings show library services and stuff.#This remains vague.
~The headings are there for services.#This is unclear.
=The headings clarify the report's structure by separating each library service into its own section.#Correct. This improves clarity and precision.
~The headings are good because they help.#This is too general.
}
::ENG3_U04_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence best uses academic vocabulary to show subject understanding? {
~The article is about murals being cool for schools.#This is informal.
~The article has important things about art.#This is vague.
~The article talks about history and pictures.#This is basic summary.
=The article frames school murals as public texts that preserve neighborhood history.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
}
